      "text": "## Totipotency"
    },
    {
      "id": "03-TISSUE-CULTURE.md#007",
      "path": [
        "03-TISSUE-CULTURE.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:horticulture:03-tissue-culture, git-history:horticulture:03-tissue-culture]",
        "canonical_path": "horticulture/03-TISSUE-CULTURE.md",
        "concepts": "[tissue, culture]",
        "current_path": "horticulture/03-TISSUE-CULTURE.md",
        "id": "maxim:horticulture:tissue-culture",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "horticulture",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[tissue, culture]",
        "section": "horticulture",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Tissue Culture and Micropropagation",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "The concept that any plant cell retains the complete genome and, in principle, can develop into a complete plant:"
    },
    {
      "id": "03-TISSUE-CULTURE.md#008",
      "path": [
        "03-TISSUE-CULTURE.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:horticulture:03-tissue-culture, git-history:horticulture:03-tissue-culture]",
        "canonical_path": "horticulture/03-TISSUE-CULTURE.md",
        "concepts": "[tissue, culture]",
        "current_path": "horticulture/03-TISSUE-CULTURE.md",
        "id": "maxim:horticulture:tissue-culture",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "horticulture",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[tissue, culture]",
        "section": "horticulture",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Tissue Culture and Micropropagation",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "```\nHISTORICAL DEVELOPMENT:\n  1902: Gottlieb Haberlandt (Austrian botanist) hypothesized that\n        isolated plant cells could be grown in culture media\n        (\"totipotency\" — from Latin totus = all).\n        His experiments failed (technical limitations)."
    },
    {
      "id": "03-TISSUE-CULTURE.md#009",
      "path": [
        "03-TISSUE-CULTURE.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:horticulture:03-tissue-culture, git-history:horticulture:03-tissue-culture]",
        "canonical_path": "horticulture/03-TISSUE-CULTURE.md",
        "concepts": "[tissue, culture]",
        "current_path": "horticulture/03-TISSUE-CULTURE.md",
        "id": "maxim:horticulture:tissue-culture",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "horticulture",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[tissue, culture]",
        "section": "horticulture",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Tissue Culture and Micropropagation",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "1950s: Folke Skoog (University of Wisconsin) developed MS medium\n         and discovered cytokinins while studying tobacco cultures.\n  1958: F.C. Steward demonstrated whole carrot plants regenerated\n         from single cells suspended in coconut milk culture.\n         First experimental confirmation of Haberlandt's hypothesis."
    },
    {
      "id": "03-TISSUE-CULTURE.md#010",
      "path": [
        "03-TISSUE-CULTURE.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:horticulture:03-tissue-culture, git-history:horticulture:03-tissue-culture]",
        "canonical_path": "horticulture/03-TISSUE-CULTURE.md",
        "concepts": "[tissue, culture]",
        "current_path": "horticulture/03-TISSUE-CULTURE.md",
        "id": "maxim:horticulture:tissue-culture",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "horticulture",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[tissue, culture]",
        "section": "horticulture",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Tissue Culture and Micropropagation",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "WHY PLANTS (BUT NOT ANIMALS):\n  Animal cells differentiate and lose pluripotency (mostly).\n  Plant cells retain totipotency because:\n  1. Plant development is post-embryonic and modular — meristems\n     produce new organs throughout life.\n  2. Wounding response requires dedifferentiation (callus).\n     The wound response mechanism can be hijacked in culture.\n  3. Plant growth regulators (auxin, cytokinin) control the\n     differentiation pathway; changing their ratio redirects fate."
    },
    {
      "id": "03-TISSUE-CULTURE.md#011",
      "path": [
        "03-TISSUE-CULTURE.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:horticulture:03-tissue-culture, git-history:horticulture:03-tissue-culture]",
        "canonical_path": "horticulture/03-TISSUE-CULTURE.md",
        "concepts": "[tissue, culture]",
        "current_path": "horticulture/03-TISSUE-CULTURE.md",
        "id": "maxim:horticulture:tissue-culture",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "horticulture",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[tissue, culture]",
        "section": "horticulture",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Tissue Culture and Micropropagation",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "NOTE: Animal cells DO retain totipotency — demonstrated by\n  Dolly the sheep (1996) somatic cell nuclear transfer.\n  But in situ plant cells are more readily reprogrammed\n  without nuclear transfer.\n```"
    },
    {
