      "text": "## Murashige-Skoog (MS) Medium"
    },
    {
      "id": "03-TISSUE-CULTURE.md#014",
      "path": [
        "03-TISSUE-CULTURE.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:horticulture:03-tissue-culture, git-history:horticulture:03-tissue-culture]",
        "canonical_path": "horticulture/03-TISSUE-CULTURE.md",
        "concepts": "[tissue, culture]",
        "current_path": "horticulture/03-TISSUE-CULTURE.md",
        "id": "maxim:horticulture:tissue-culture",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "horticulture",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[tissue, culture]",
        "section": "horticulture",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Tissue Culture and Micropropagation",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "Toshio Murashige and Folke Skoog (1962) developed the medium that became the standard formulation for plant tissue culture:"
    },
    {
      "id": "03-TISSUE-CULTURE.md#015",
      "path": [
        "03-TISSUE-CULTURE.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:horticulture:03-tissue-culture, git-history:horticulture:03-tissue-culture]",
        "canonical_path": "horticulture/03-TISSUE-CULTURE.md",
        "concepts": "[tissue, culture]",
        "current_path": "horticulture/03-TISSUE-CULTURE.md",
        "id": "maxim:horticulture:tissue-culture",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "horticulture",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[tissue, culture]",
        "section": "horticulture",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Tissue Culture and Micropropagation",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "```\nMS MEDIUM COMPONENTS:\n  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n  │ MACRONUTRIENTS (mM range):                                      │\n  │   NH₄NO₃ (ammonium nitrate)    1650 mg/L — nitrogen source      │\n  │   KNO₃ (potassium nitrate)      1900 mg/L — nitrogen + potassium│\n  │   CaCl₂·2H₂O                    440 mg/L  — calcium             │\n  │   MgSO₄·7H₂O                    370 mg/L  — magnesium + sulfur  │\n  │   KH₂PO₄                        170 mg/L  — phosphorus          │\n  ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n  │ MICRONUTRIENTS (μM range):                                       │\n  │   MnSO₄, ZnSO₄, H₃BO₃, KI,                                     │\n  │   Na₂MoO₄, CuSO₄, CoCl₂, FeSO₄/Na₂EDTA                        │\n  │   (exact concentrations vary by species and purpose)             │\n  ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n  │ VITAMINS:                                                       │\n  │   Myo-inositol (100 mg/L) — cell wall synthesis                 │\n  │   Thiamine (B1), Pyridoxine (B6), Nicotinic acid                │\n  ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n  │ CARBON SOURCE:                                                   │\n  │   Sucrose (30 g/L) — plants in culture are heterotrophic;       │\n  │   they do photosynthesize but not enough for growth;            │\n  │   external carbon required                                       │\n  ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n  │ GELLING AGENT (for solid media):                                │\n  │   Agar (6–8 g/L) — seaweed polysaccharide; gels at 40°C         │\n  │   Alternative: Gelrite (gellan gum) — clearer, more defined     │\n  ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n  │ PLANT GROWTH REGULATORS (variable):                             │\n  │   Auxin (IAA, IBA, 2,4-D, NAA) — concentration varies          │\n  │   Cytokinin (BAP, kinetin, zeatin) — concentration varies       │\n  │   See Skoog-Miller ratio below                                  │\n  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n  Final pH: 5.7–5.8 (before autoclaving; drops slightly after)\n  Autoclave: 121°C, 15 PSI, 15–20 minutes → sterilized\n```"
    }
  ],
